Build the shared foundational layer, Oracle system, data models, goal-directed intent engine, reporting standards, Windows packaging, and acceptance infrastructure that both the four core detection engines and the application shell depend on. This is the critical “glue and quality” layer that turns the engines from noisy scanners into high-signal, outcome-focused tools. Everything in this prompt must be production-ready and designed for October 2026 realities.

### 1. Central Oracle & Side-Effect Verification System (the heart of signal quality)

This is the single most important shared component. No finding is ever raised unless the Oracle confirms a real, observable unauthorized state change.

**Core responsibilities**
- Maintain a live, per-context snapshot of all relevant application state:
  - Wallet / balance / points / credit values
  - Order status, payment status, fulfillment status
  - Entitlements, licenses, download links, redemption flags
  - Inventory or quantity counters where visible
- Provide fast before/after capture APIs that any engine can call:
  - `oracle.captureState(contextId, resourceHints)`
  - `oracle.diff(before, after)` → structured delta
- Only allow a finding to be emitted when the delta shows a clear unauthorized benefit (balance increased without corresponding verified payment, order moved to fulfilled/paid without payment, new entitlement appeared, etc.).
- Support both explicit polling (re-fetch balance/order endpoints) and passive observation (detect state changes from traffic the browser already made).
- Handle delayed side-effects (some systems credit balance or fulfill asynchronously) with configurable wait-and-recheck windows.
- Multi-context awareness: the Oracle must track state separately for each authenticated browser context (userA, userB, etc.) so the Authz Matrix Runner can prove cross-account impact.
- Resilience: if a state endpoint is temporarily unavailable or rate-limited, the Oracle retries intelligently and falls back to alternative signals (e.g., presence of a download link, change in UI text, new GraphQL node).

**Design requirements**
- Pluggable state extractors so new applications can teach the Oracle how to read their particular balance or order shape (JSONPath, CSS selectors for rendered values, GraphQL field paths, etc.).
- High performance: state capture must be fast enough to run before and after high-concurrency races.
- Clear, structured delta objects that are directly embeddable in evidence packs.

### 2. Shared Resource Inventory & Data Models

A single source of truth used by discovery, all four engines, the Oracle, and the UI.

**Must track**
- Endpoints (URL, method, GraphQL operation name, parameters, authentication requirements, category tags: wallet / payment / order / fulfill / etc.)
- Resource identifiers (type, example values per context, format: UUID / sequential / etc.)
- Parameters of interest (especially monetary and quantity fields) with observed types and example values
- Observed state shapes (how balance, order status, entitlements appear in responses or in the DOM)
- Relationships (this orderId belongs to this userId, this payment references this order, etc.)

**Requirements**
- Continuously updated from live traffic and from engine activity.
- Queryable by any engine (“give me all credit-related endpoints”, “give me all orderIds belonging to context B”, “give me all parameters that look like amounts”).
- Persistent across scan pauses and restorable from saved sessions.
- Efficient in-memory + optional disk-backed storage for long scans.

### 3. Goal-Directed Intent Engine

Users are non-experts. They think in outcomes, not vulnerability classes.

**Capabilities**
- Accept plain-language or structured intents, for example:
  - “Increase my balance / wallet / points without paying”
  - “Get this item / unlock this content / fulfill an order without payment”
  - “Transfer value from another account to mine”
- Map the intent to the most relevant engines, endpoints, parameters, and test strategies.
- Dynamically raise the priority of certain tests and lower others.
- Surface progress in intent terms (“Testing 14 balance-credit paths…”, “Checking whether orders can be fulfilled without payment confirmation…”).
- At the end of the scan, present findings grouped and explained according to the original intent.

### 4. Reporting & Evidence Standards

Every finding must be immediately usable for a bug-bounty report or an internal ticket.

**Mandatory evidence pack contents**
- Clear title and short description tied to outcome A (unauthorized credit) or B (unpaid fulfillment)
- Severity rationale
- Full HTTP request and response (with sensitive tokens redacted in the exported version if desired)
- Before/after Oracle state diff
- Exact parameters or IDs that were manipulated
- Step-by-step reproduction instructions that work with the saved browser contexts
- Timestamps and engine that discovered it
- Optional: screenshots of balance or order pages, timing diagrams for races

**Output formats**
- Structured JSON (machine-readable, complete)
- Clean HTML report (human-readable, suitable for sharing)
- Optional Markdown and ZIP (JSON + evidence files)

Findings from all four engines are aggregated, lightly deduplicated, and presented both as a flat list and grouped by the two business outcomes.

### 5. Windows Packaging, First-Run Experience & Operational Robustness

- Simple, professional Windows installer or portable executable.
- First-run wizard: explain what the tool does, what the user needs to do (log in), and what the tool will never do (it only uses the session the user provides).
- Clear handling of common Windows issues (firewall prompts, antivirus false positives, missing Visual C++ redistributables, etc.).
- Automatic cleanup of controlled browser processes and temporary profiles on exit or crash.
- Optional encrypted local storage for saved sessions and findings.
- Logging that is useful for debugging without overwhelming a non-expert user.
- Graceful degradation: if Playwright/Chromium fails to launch, give a clear actionable error instead of a stack trace.

### 6. Acceptance & Quality Infrastructure

- Built-in mutation-testing style self-check mode (for developers of the tool): the ability to inject known vulnerable patterns (missing authz check, double-credit race, amount mismatch accepted, client-controlled price trusted) and verify that the corresponding engine + Oracle combination detects them.
- Clear internal interfaces and contracts between:
  - Browser / session layer
  - Traffic pipeline
  - Resource inventory
  - Oracle
  - Each of the four engines
  - UI / reporting
- Comprehensive but practical test suite covering the Oracle, inventory, intent mapping, and evidence generation.
- Documentation of every public API so the four engines and the shell can be developed and evolved independently.

### Design principles for this entire layer
- High signal above all else — the Oracle is the primary filter against noise.
- Non-expert friendly language and progress reporting.
- Everything works from the authenticated browser context only; no assumption of server-side access, database credentials, or privileged APIs.
- Modular and versionable so engines can be improved without rewriting the foundation.
- Production quality: proper error handling, resource cleanup, and resilience to real-world flaky applications.

Deliver a complete, robust, production-ready implementation of this shared foundation. It must make the four detection engines dramatically more effective and the overall application trustworthy and easy to use. This layer is what turns raw detection capability into reliable, outcome-focused findings that a non-expert can actually act on.
